WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.540
Every test suite of any size has the same problem.

00:00:03.180 --> 00:00:05.600
You can run all of it, or you can run one thing.

00:00:06.280 --> 00:00:11.960
Running exactly the right part, and being able to prove afterwards which part that was, is the awkward bit.

00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:13.860
Say it is Tuesday.

00:00:14.620 --> 00:00:16.540
Something changed in the checkout code this morning.

00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:20.000
Tonight you want payments, checkout, and the catalog.

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:22.980
Not the tests the team already knows are broken.

00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:29.640
And tomorrow, when somebody asks what you actually ran, you would like a better answer than the nightly job.

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:31.800
Tags help with that.

00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:37.260
Until the tag query ends up buried in a build server setting that nobody can reconstruct three weeks later.

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:39.500
This is runcomposer.

00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:41.000
Let me just show you.

00:00:42.167 --> 00:00:48.867
Here it is. On the left, the areas of the shop. Payments, Checkout, Cart, Catalog, Accounts.

00:00:49.447 --> 00:00:51.307
That list is not built into the tool.

00:00:51.887 --> 00:00:57.387
Somebody on the team wrote it down, in a plain file, out of the labels the tests already carry.

00:00:58.327 --> 00:01:05.647
Click Payments. Underneath, immediately the 13 tests carrying that label, by name, with their labels beside them.

00:01:05.647 --> 00:01:14.687
Click Checkout as well, and the count drops to zero, because right now it is asking for tests that are payments and checkout, and no test is both.

00:01:15.327 --> 00:01:18.247
Change the little word in the middle to OR and there they are.

00:01:18.867 --> 00:01:21.387
26. Add the catalog, 34.

00:01:22.327 --> 00:01:24.107
Now leave the broken ones out.

00:01:24.727 --> 00:01:26.707
Two of these 34 are in quarantine.

00:01:27.547 --> 00:01:31.967
Everybody knows they fail, somebody is fixing them, and they are not the point tonight.

00:01:32.647 --> 00:01:33.207
Untick them.

00:01:34.047 --> 00:01:35.087
32 tests.

00:01:35.647 --> 00:01:37.047
And here's the bit worth noticing.

00:01:37.647 --> 00:01:40.567
You can read all 32 right now before anything runs.

00:01:41.147 --> 00:01:41.827
Not a number.

00:01:42.227 --> 00:01:43.147
The actual names.

00:01:44.267 --> 00:01:47.087
Give the run a name, you will thank yourself in the morning.

00:01:47.687 --> 00:01:48.587
Then press play.

00:01:49.587 --> 00:01:53.687
It is already on the runs page, marked running, and the badge is counting.

00:01:54.407 --> 00:01:56.547
18 of 32 verdicts in, then 25.

00:01:57.367 --> 00:01:59.227
Then a pause while the slow ones finish.

00:02:00.207 --> 00:02:03.747
Those results arrive while the tests are still going, one at a time,

00:02:04.167 --> 00:02:06.347
each with the name of the test that just finished.

00:02:06.927 --> 00:02:09.107
Green if it passed, red if it did not.

00:02:09.767 --> 00:02:14.547
Underneath it says the work went out in two parts, side by side, so it finishes sooner.

00:02:15.547 --> 00:02:18.747
And when both parts have reported back, the run says complete.

00:02:19.307 --> 00:02:20.267
Not probably done.

00:02:20.847 --> 00:02:23.487
It knew how many parts it was owed, and it got them all.

00:02:24.427 --> 00:02:25.327
31 passed.

00:02:25.827 --> 00:02:26.407
One failed.

00:02:27.067 --> 00:02:29.727
An expired card was accepted when it should have been rejected.

00:02:30.207 --> 00:02:32.867
There it is in red, with the reason on the line.

00:02:34.067 --> 00:02:36.607
Now the part that makes this more than a convenient button.

00:02:37.587 --> 00:02:42.227
When you pressed play, runcomposer wrote the plan down first, a small file

00:02:42.307 --> 00:02:43.327
holding three things.

00:02:43.847 --> 00:02:48.407
The rule you built in the words you built it in, the exact list of 32 test

00:02:48.407 --> 00:02:52.727
names, and a fingerprint of the whole set of tests taken at that moment.

00:02:53.687 --> 00:02:55.127
Each one earns its place.

00:02:55.687 --> 00:02:57.647
The rule so anyone can see what you meant.

00:02:58.227 --> 00:03:01.127
The list so what runs is fixed and cannot drift.

00:03:01.667 --> 00:03:02.487
The fingerprint.

00:03:02.967 --> 00:03:06.707
So if the tests change afterwards, that gets noticed instead of discovered.

00:03:07.627 --> 00:03:09.707
Which is why the run does not have to happen here.

00:03:10.267 --> 00:03:11.787
The other button says export.

00:03:12.327 --> 00:03:14.127
Same selection, same list.

00:03:14.547 --> 00:03:16.007
Only now you get the file.

00:03:16.947 --> 00:03:17.927
Hand it to anything.

00:03:18.307 --> 00:03:19.147
A build job.

00:03:19.547 --> 00:03:20.227
A colleague.

00:03:20.767 --> 00:03:24.347
A machine in a lab this tool cannot reach and has no password for.

00:03:24.887 --> 00:03:29.207
Over there, one small Python file reads it, runs your usual test command

00:03:29.207 --> 00:03:32.327
against exactly those names and writes the results beside them.

00:03:33.267 --> 00:03:35.567
Bring the results back by any route at all.

00:03:36.007 --> 00:03:40.427
And they land on this run because the file carried its own reference number the whole way.

00:03:41.387 --> 00:03:44.047
The interface is a document, not a protocol.

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:47.420
Next morning, one test failed.

00:03:47.960 --> 00:03:49.880
You do not want to run 32 again.

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:51.200
You want that one.

00:03:52.160 --> 00:03:53.680
At the top, there is a shortcut.

00:03:54.120 --> 00:03:56.000
Rerun what failed in the latest run.

00:03:56.540 --> 00:03:59.480
One click and the selection is exactly that test.

00:03:59.980 --> 00:04:00.620
One of one.

00:04:01.160 --> 00:04:03.740
And the new run keeps a note of where its list came from.

00:04:04.700 --> 00:04:07.040
Latest means latest, finished by the way.

00:04:07.620 --> 00:04:11.100
A run still waiting on results is not quietly treated as an answer.

00:04:12.060 --> 00:04:12.740
One more thing.

00:04:12.740 --> 00:04:17.540
If results turn up that match no run you asked for, they do not join your history.

00:04:18.280 --> 00:04:20.860
They wait here, in the open, with the reason.

00:04:21.540 --> 00:04:22.140
You decide.

00:04:22.640 --> 00:04:24.580
Attach, promote, or discard.

00:04:25.640 --> 00:04:26.680
That is runcomposer.

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:28.020
Open source.

00:04:28.520 --> 00:04:28.900
MIT.

00:04:29.420 --> 00:04:30.400
Running on your own machine.

00:04:31.480 --> 00:04:33.220
Pick exactly the tests you mean.

00:04:33.760 --> 00:04:34.420
Keep the receipt.
